.TH AXSOCKS 1 "15 September 2026" "axcall" "Linux Programmer's Manual"
.SH NAME
axsocks \- SOCKS5 proxy onto an AX.25 port
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B axsocks
.RI [ options ]
.I port
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B axsocks
puts a SOCKS5 proxy in front of one AX.25 port. Every connection it accepts
becomes a connected-mode AX.25 session to a callsign, and the bytes pass
through untouched in both directions.
.PP
The point is that nothing downstream has to know what AX.25 is. SOCKS5 is
spoken by
.BR curl (1),
.BR ssh (1),
browsers and every HTTP library in wide use, so a program in any language can
reach a packet station through an ordinary socket.
.PP
The mapping is exact rather than approximate. A connected-mode AX.25 session is
reliable, ordered and flow-controlled, and so is a TCP connection, so there is
nothing to reconcile between them: the proxy opens a link, copies bytes, and
closes the link when the bytes run out.
.PP
It needs no kernel support, no
.BR kissattach (8)
and no privilege, because it carries its own AX.25 v2.2 data link and speaks
KISS to a TNC over a serial port or over TCP, exactly as
.BR axcall (1)
does.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.I port
The AX.25 port to call out over: a name from the ports file, a device path, or
.IR host : port
for a KISS-over-TCP modem. See
.BR axcall (1)
for the ports file.
.TP
.BI \-s " callsign" "\fR, \fP" \-\-mycall " callsign"
The callsign to call out as. Required unless the ports file entry supplies one.
.TP
.BI \-\-listen " spec"
Where to accept SOCKS5 connections: a bare port, or
.IR address : port .
The default is
.BR 127.0.0.1:1080 ,
and the address half of that default is deliberate. Binding this to an address
the rest of the network can reach hands anyone who can open the port the use of
your callsign and your transmitter.
.TP
.BI \-\-hosts " file"
Read this hosts file instead of searching the usual paths.
.TP
.BI \-\-serial " device" [: baud ]
.TQ
.BI \-\-tcp " host" : port
Name the transport directly instead of giving a
.IR port .
.TP
.BI \-\-baud " rate"
Serial baud rate. Overrides a rate from the ports file. The default is 57600.
.TP
.BI \-p " bytes" "\fR, \fP" \-\-paclen " bytes"
Maximum information field per frame (N1), 1 to 256.
.TP
.BI \-w " frames" "\fR, \fP" \-\-window " frames"
Send window (k), 1 to 7, or 1 to 63 with
.BR \-\-mod128 .
.TP
.B \-\-mod128
Prefer AX.25 v2.2 (SABME, modulo 128) on each call.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-xid
Skip the pre-connect XID exchange that negotiates selective reject.
.TP
.BI \-\-keepalive " seconds"
T3, the inactive-link timer. The default is 300.
.TP
.BI \-\-txdelay " ms"
.TQ
.BI \-\-persist " 0-255"
.TQ
.BI \-\-slottime " ms"
.TQ
.BI \-\-txtail " ms"
KISS channel-access parameters, as
.BR kissparms (8)
set them per interface. Timers are in milliseconds and must be a multiple of
10, the KISS unit.
.TP
.B \-d
Trace every frame, sent and received, to standard error.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Log errors only, rather than a line per connection.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage and exit.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print the version, and the versions of the libraries built in.
.SH HOSTS FILE
A SOCKS client asks for a hostname. The hosts file turns that into a callsign:
.PP
.RS
.nf
# name     callsign    port
gb7rdg     GB7RDG      radio
gb7cip     GB7CIP\-1    radio
.fi
.RE
.PP
It is read from
.I /etc/axcall/hosts
and then
.IR ~/.config/axcall/hosts ,
the second replacing same-named entries in the first, or from the single file
named by
.BR \-\-hosts " or " $AXCALL_HOSTS .
Names are matched without regard to case.
.PP
A name that is not listed but is itself a valid callsign is used as one, so
.B gb7rdg\-1
works with no configuration at all. An empty hosts file is a perfectly good
hosts file.
.SH USING IT
The client has to let
.B axsocks
resolve the name. Given an address instead, there is nothing useful to be done
with it: a callsign is not an IP address and no mapping back exists, so the
request is refused rather than guessed at.
.PP
.RS
.nf
axsocks \-\-serial /dev/ttyUSB0:57600 \-s M0LTE\-7 radio

curl \-\-socks5\-hostname localhost:1080 http://gb7rdg/status
ssh \-o ProxyCommand='nc \-X 5 \-x localhost:1080 %h %p' pi@lab
.fi
.RE
.PP
The
.B \-\-socks5\-hostname
and
.B "\-X 5 \-x"
forms are what push resolution to the proxy;
.B \-\-socks5
and a plain
.B \-x
do not.
.SH LIMITS
Streams only. There is no UDP, no ICMP and no ping, because a connected-mode
AX.25 session carries a byte stream and nothing else.
.PP
The port number in a SOCKS request is read and ignored. A callsign is the whole
address, and a station decides what to run for a caller by which of its
callsigns was called, not by a port number.
.PP
One connection at a time per station. An AX.25 session is a single ordered
stream between a pair of callsigns, so a second call to a station already in
session would share the first one's link and interleave with it; it is refused
instead. Bear this in mind before pointing a browser at the proxy, which will
cheerfully open six connections at once.
.PP
Every connection costs a SABM/UA round trip and holds a session open for its
lifetime. On a shared half-duplex channel at 1200 baud that is a real cost.
This suits one long-lived connection to a station far better than it suits
chatty modern HTTP.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Stopped cleanly.
.TP
.B 1
Something unexpected went wrong.
.TP
.B 2
The command line was wrong.
.TP
.B 3
The modem could not be opened, or the listening port could not be bound.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/axcall/hosts
System hosts file.
.TP
.I ~/.config/axcall/hosts
Per-user hosts file, read second.
.TP
.I /etc/axcall/ports
System ports file, shared with
.BR axcall (1).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B AXCALL_HOSTS
If set, the only hosts file consulted.
.TP
.B AXCALL_PORTS
If set, the only ports file consulted.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR axcall (1),
.BR curl (1),
.BR ssh (1)
.PP
